speechtext:
Madam Speaker, following consultation with the other parties and with unanimous consent of the House, I wish to move the following order of reference:
That the Standing Committee on Transport, to complement its consideration of transportation matters, while travelling in eastern and western Canada during the month of September 1980, be empowered to examine and report on federal transportation policies and programs within central Canada;
That the committee have the power to retain the services of expert, professional, technical, clerical and stenographic staff as may be deemed necessary;
That for the purpose of such examination, the committee be authorized to travel from place to place in Ontario and Quebec for a period of up to 15 days during the time of the 1981 summer adjournment of the first session of the thirty-second Parliament; and that the necessary staff do accompany.

(a) The term "attended" is used when a
June 30, 1981

Coast Guard resource arrives at or is dispatched to the scene of a marine incident and undertakes:
(1)	to render direct assistance;
(2)	to advise and co-ordinate assistance already in progress;
(3)	to relay communications on behalf of RCC and/or other vessels involved;
(4)	to standby when pollutants may be discharged during the course of an incident.
(b)	The first figure of $900 is based on the average cost per incident of CG 122 during its first six months of operation. The second figure of $1,273.59 is the average cost per incident of all Coast Guard Search and Rescue resources on the west coast, including seasonal inshore rescue boats (IRB's).
(c)	In the North Saanich/Gulf Islands area, incidents are generally close to shore and seldom involve more than one auxiliary vessel. The average time spent on an incident in this area is about one hour. The figure of $20 is based on the reimbursement fee for out-of-pocket expenses by volunteers and does not include insurance charges which the Coast Guard pays.
In other areas along the B.C. coast, the average time to resolve a marine SAR incident is much longer and normally requires the participation of more than one auxiliary craft. In addition, auxiliary vessels outside the Gulf Islands/Greater Vancouver area are generally larger and more expensive to operate. The figure of $247.94 given in response to question 2,126 (3(c)), 17/02/81, includes the cost of insurance premiums paid by the government for each auxiliary incident.
